        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(update): prevent Gateway respawn during package replacement",
        "body": "Closes #103447\nRelated: #85133\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where background auto-updates could replace an npm-global OpenClaw package while launchd had already respawned a Gateway from that package. The same gap is possible during explicitly marked managed-update handoffs on systemd or Task Scheduler.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nTreat a loaded, mutation-relevant Darwin LaunchAgent as respawn-pending because its shipped plist uses `KeepAlive`; an operator `bootout` is not loaded. On Linux and Windows, require the detached managed-update handoff marker before treating a stopped-but-loaded service as transient. The updater awaits the existing cross-platform stop path before package or git mutation, without timing sleeps, while ordinary stopped systemd units and Scheduled Tasks retain their prior behavior.\n\n## User Impact\n\nManaged updates no longer risk mixing old in-memory Gateway code with newly replaced package or build files during supervisor restart windows. The service still restarts through the existing successful-update and failure-recovery paths.\n\n## Release Note Context\n\nThe centralized release changelog owns the #103447 entry; this PR intentionally carries only the runtime fix and regression coverage.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Before fix, exact macOS 26.5 npm-global package proof reproduced the race: original PID 10120 exited, launchd respawned baseline PID 10903, and the target package swap occurred while PID 10903 remained alive before and after mutation. Baseline `a24139e19976f79e0a14a5a77a3f38794396c6ef`; target `b6a86b34cea1618aa5233249af36e96d3d1ce2fb`.\n- Regression tests hold `service.stop` unresolved for Darwin and explicit Linux handoff paths, prove no npm mutation starts, release the stop, then prove mutation ordering and post-install restoration. Companion cases preserve unloaded Darwin and ordinary stopped Linux/Windows behavior, including no failure-recovery restart intent.\n- Blacksmith Testbox through Crabbox lease `tbx_01kx5dqppd7g62r6kja2k5qyma`: `src/cli/update-cli.test.ts` passed 186/186; scoped `check:changed` passed core/coreTests typecheck, lint, and guards.\n- Targeted `oxfmt --check` passed for the implementation and test files.\n- Fresh Codex autoreview: clean, no accepted/actionable findings; overall correct (0.91).\n- Cross-platform owner contracts reviewed: a loaded launchd job uses `KeepAlive` and stop uses `bootout`; systemd and Scheduled Task stopped states are ambiguous without the update-handoff marker, and their existing stop/suspension paths remain the quiescence owners once that marker proves restart intent.\n",
